# Mutating projected source safely

A read-only answer can be omitted when its mapping is uncertain. A proposed source
change needs a stronger guarantee: every accepted edit must mean the same thing in
the literate source as it meant in the projected file.

Literate exposes only explicitly requested, edit-only actions from the language
server. An action that declares a kind must belong to the quick-fix or refactoring
families; the protocol also permits an otherwise safe action to omit its kind. The server proposes an edit; Literate validates and maps
the complete proposal; the editor presents the resulting action; and the user
decides whether to apply it. The language-service adapter never applies the change
by itself.

## One safe edit is not enough

A workspace edit is a set. If a server proposes three coordinated replacements and
only two map safely, applying the two would create a fourth program that neither the
server nor the author intended. Literate therefore treats the entire action as one
atomic validation unit.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    Reply[Server action reply] --> Explicit{Explicit user invocation?}
    Explicit -->|No| Reject[Offer no action]
    Explicit -->|Yes| Fresh{Exact source snapshot still current?}
    Fresh -->|No| Reject
    Fresh -->|Yes| Shape{Supported edit-only shape?}
    Shape -->|No| Reject
    Shape -->|Yes| Complete{Every edit maps through one safe occurrence?}
    Complete -->|No| Reject
    Complete -->|Yes| Order{Non-overlapping deterministic order?}
    Order -->|No| Reject
    Order -->|Yes| Offer[Offer mapped editor action]
```

Every rejection arrow discards the whole action, not merely the questionable edit.
The diagram omits optional display metadata because metadata never widens edit
authority.

## Freshness is checked again at reply time

The request records the open source document, its editor revision, the source path,
the projected file, the exact mapping occurrence, and the generated URI sent to the
server. When a reply arrives, Literate verifies all of them again.

The document must still be open at the same revision. The mapping object must still
belong to the projected file. Its source and generated intervals must be valid,
equal in length, and within their respective texts. Most importantly, the captured
source text and generated text for that interval must still contain identical
UTF-16 code-unit sequences.

This text equality is stronger than trusting positions alone. A stale interval of
the same length could otherwise pass numerical bounds while containing different
code.

Only an explicit editor invocation reaches this path. Automatically requested
actions are rejected before snapshot capture, so background activity cannot turn a
server suggestion into a source-changing option.

## Mutation is confined to one projection segment

Every returned text edit must map through the exact occurrence used for the request.
It cannot cross into an adjacent mapping, even when that mapping comes from the same
source document. It cannot touch wrapper text, inserted indentation, a reference
seam, or another generated occurrence.

The selected source interval must also have exactly one matching occurrence in the
projected file used for the request. If that same interval appears twice there,
changing the single source interval would alter both generated occurrences, while
the server's edit may describe only one. Literate rejects that ambiguity.

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    Source[One editable source interval] --> A[Generated occurrence A]
    Source --> B[Generated occurrence B]
    Server[Server proposes change to A] --> Proof{Exactly one matching occurrence in requested file?}
    A --> Proof
    B --> Proof
    Proof -->|No| Reject[Reject the action]
```

The diagram shows why text equality is insufficient: both occurrences may contain
the same text but have different generated roles. The implemented uniqueness proof
is scoped to the projected file that received the request. It does not claim global
uniqueness across every projected output.

This narrow segment rule also explains why mutation is stricter than diagnostic
mapping. Diagnostics may split a range across several honest source intersections.
An edit is not split, because doing so could change its semantics.

## Only one plain edit form crosses the boundary

The protocol permits many kinds of workspace change. Literate accepts only the
plain `changes` form with exactly one entry, and that entry must name the generated
URI used for the request. Its value must be a nonempty bounded list of ordinary text
edits.

The following forms fail closed:

- changes to another generated or physical file;
- document-change records and file operations;
- annotated edits or snippet-shaped edits;
- commands, disabled actions, or actions requiring later resolution;
- empty, oversized, malformed, or partly mappable edit sets;
- declared action kinds outside quick fixes and refactorings.

Replacement text may be ordinary multiline text, but the aggregate replacement size
is bounded. The number of actions and edits is bounded as well. Exceeding a limit
rejects the proposal rather than truncating it.

This boundary intentionally excludes useful-looking behavior such as a local
completion accompanied by an import change. Safety is determined by the complete
shape, not by whether the first edit appears harmless.

## Overlap makes order part of meaning

Two edits overlap when applying one changes the coordinates or source bytes expected
by the other. Even two zero-width insertions at the same position have an order that
may affect the result. A replacement and insertion sharing a start have the same
problem.

Literate first retains and maps the complete edit set, then sorts it by source start
and end. It rejects overlaps and any pair sharing a start. Only after those checks
can the editor's workspace-edit representation preserve the server's intended set
without relying on an implicit application order.

## Optional metadata has no edit authority

An action may carry diagnostics explaining why it was offered. Literate keeps only
a bounded portable subset when the complete diagnostic list is valid and maps
through the same occurrence. Message, severity, code, source label, and standard
tags can be retained. Links, related locations, private data, and unknown extension
fields are not.

Malformed diagnostic metadata does not make an otherwise safe edit unsafe. In that
case Literate offers the action without the metadata. By contrast, a malformed edit
or executable command rejects the whole action because it changes what the action
could do.

At the top level, the server reply must be an array of valid actions. A bare command
or one malformed action causes the complete reply to be rejected. Silently filtering
a mutating reply could change the set or ordering that the server intended to
present.

Safe mutation over projections is therefore a proof obligation, not a convenience:
current source, exact occurrence, complete edit set, supported shape, and
unambiguous order must all agree. Chapter 23 places that proof inside the wider
editor coordinator that schedules snapshots, sessions, automatic requests, and
background diagnostics.
